WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.420
Press the skin of a mouse's hindpaw.

2
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.880
How does the news reach the brainstem?

3
00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:10.240
The textbook draws one route.

4
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.100
There are two.

5
00:00:12.683 --> 00:00:14.283
Start with the textbook drawing.

6
00:00:15.123 --> 00:00:20.223
A touch afferent, an Aβ low-threshold mechanoreceptor, enters the spinal cord.

7
00:00:21.163 --> 00:00:24.143
Its main branch climbs the dorsal column on the same side.

8
00:00:25.117 --> 00:00:29.497
It reaches the gracile nucleus in the brainstem with no synapse on the way.

9
00:00:30.477 --> 00:00:31.577
Call this the direct route.

10
00:00:32.517 --> 00:00:34.077
Now look below that drawing.

11
00:00:34.937 --> 00:00:39.237
The same afferent also sends branches into the dorsal horn, a layered circuit.

12
00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:42.850
Those branches drive local interneurons.

13
00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:48.350
Through them, and in places directly, they drive a spinal neuron whose own axon

14
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:50.010
climbs the same dorsal column.

15
00:00:51.033 --> 00:00:55.393
This is a postsynaptic dorsal column neuron, or PSDC neuron.

16
00:00:56.253 --> 00:00:59.593
It is driven in the dorsal horn and projects to the brainstem.

17
00:01:00.617 --> 00:01:05.317
Recordings in cats described these neurons by the early 1980s.

18
00:01:05.997 --> 00:01:08.917
Mouse work adds genetic access, and a test of each route.

19
00:01:09.867 --> 00:01:14.607
So draw a second line, in gold, from the afferent through the circuit and up to the

20
00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:15.607
gracile nucleus.

21
00:01:16.517 --> 00:01:17.717
Pause and predict.

22
00:01:18.777 --> 00:01:22.597
Does the gold line end on the same brainstem neurons as the blue line?

23
00:01:28.617 --> 00:01:31.337
For thalamus-projecting gracile neurons, yes.

24
00:01:32.277 --> 00:01:34.937
The two inputs had highly aligned receptive fields.

25
00:01:35.877 --> 00:01:37.437
Both reported the same patch of skin.

26
00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:40.370
But the two reports are not the same.

27
00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:43.070
The direct route keeps precise timing.

28
00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:47.390
The indirect route reports sustained pressure, up to high forces.

29
00:01:48.483 --> 00:01:50.343
That is the idea for this lesson.

30
00:01:51.283 --> 00:01:52.603
Two routes, one patch of skin.

31
00:01:53.503 --> 00:01:54.903
One report keeps the timing.

32
00:01:55.783 --> 00:01:58.083
The other is rebuilt in the cord, not copied.

33
00:02:02.117 --> 00:02:07.177
The limits: these are mouse studies, mostly in anaesthetized adults, and the dorsal

34
00:02:07.177 --> 00:02:08.297
column route only.

35
00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:13.580
The takeaway: one touch, two routes, two different reports on the same skin.
